Definition 1 (Conditional Expectation) Let \((\Omega, \mathcal{F}_0, P)\) be a given probability space. Let \(X\) be a \(\mathcal{F}_0\)-measurable random variable with \(\E |X| &lt; \infty\). For a given \(\sigma\)-field \(\mathcal{F} \subset \mathcal{F}_0\), the conditional expectation of \(X\) given \(\mathcal{F}\), \(\E[X \mid \mathcal{F}]\), is a random variable \(Y\) such that

\(Y\) is \(\mathcal{F}\)-measurable, and
for all \(A \in \mathcal{F}\), \(\displaystyle \int_A X dP = \int_A Y dP\).

Any \(Y\) satisfying i. and ii. is said to be a version of \(\E[X \mid \mathcal{F}]\).


Remark. For simplicity, if \(Y\) is \(\mathcal{F}\)-measurable, we commonly denote it as \(Y \in \mathcal{F}\).

